l:provider_field"},{"id":"part_a1c39aa550e6","type":"text","text":"The checked numbers show sharp particle bursts, not a smooth all-day rise. Wind was not calm at the largest burst, and CO₂ was almost as high earlier when particles were low. The figure will let you compare those periods and inspect the later, unresolved negative CO₂ readings without hiding them.","agent_id":"main","sequence":57,"stream_source":"live","channel":"next_thought"},{"id":"live_call_1099105722cb_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_1099105722cb","agent_id":"main","sequence":58,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_ff796ce64f5b_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_ff796ce64f5b","agent_id":"main","sequence":60,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_8a12d2e46183_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_8a12d2e46183","agent_id":"main","sequence":62,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_a2ui_4e86a07a2757","type":"a2ui","surface_id":"konza-burn-explorer","sequence":63},{"id":"live_call_45ad592c1f28_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_45ad592c1f28","agent_id":"main","sequence":65,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_d4821988fe82_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_d4821988fe82","agent_id":"main","sequence":66,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_31766ce7d26e_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_31766ce7d26e","agent_id":"main","sequence":69,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_0da5361c12af_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_0da5361c12af","agent_id":"main","sequence":71,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_535eeccaa07c_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_535eeccaa07c","agent_id":"main","sequence":73,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_9e0da218e67e_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_9e0da218e67e","agent_id":"main","sequence":75,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_c47020a946ea_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_c47020a946ea","agent_id":"main","sequence":78,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_679109e71f92_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_679109e71f92","agent_id":"main","sequence":80,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_5a17269c5fb2_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_5a17269c5fb2","agent_id":"main","sequence":82,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_b7d0d7c75173_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_b7d0d7c75173","agent_id":"main","sequence":85,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_a715f9bc7b42_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_a715f9bc7b42","agent_id":"main","sequence":87,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_aa9ad188baf6_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_aa9ad188baf6","agent_id":"main","sequence":89,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_09c9b94cacd3_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_09c9b94cacd3","agent_id":"main","sequence":92,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_ef4a5d48cb93_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_ef4a5d48cb93","agent_id":"main","sequence":94,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"part_12bde39678f1","type":"reasoning","started_at":"2026-10-19T18:27:57.986206+00:00","completed_at":"2026-10-19T18:28:07.515209+00:00","duration_ms":9528.998400084674,"text":"**Evaluating rendering issues**\n\nI'm wondering why the legend has disappeared from my display. It was previously at the bottom, but now it seems absent, leaving a blank screenshot. It could be that the data state isn't rendering properly, even if it's technically ready. The coverage looks fine, though. I also need to adjust the x-axis to include different time slots and create a final concise report of about 500 to 700 words, ensuring all key facts and limitations are documented.**Clarifying data reporting needs**\n\nI'm noticing that the quality point is nearly zero, but the coverage is still clearly visible. I don't think I need to save a dashboard overview right now. Should I create an artifact report in JSON format? While the user can interact in chat without a \"save\" option, I want to ensure the JSON doesn't just end up being unhelpful. I should provide a downloadable CSV instead, and there are gaps and sensor limits to annotate, specifying that timestamps may not be synchronized.**Addressing scale and labels**\n\nI think I should add a direct title regarding the particle scale issue since it's currently clipped at 10,000. The y-axis is visible with ticks labeled at 1,000, not 10,000, which seems odd since there isn't much overlap. It looks like there might be a symlog bug affecting the numeric scale, showing only certain labels. I also need to update the heading with a full definition and include a note about the scale. Lastly, I’ll restore the controls and inspect the final capture.","source":"provider","provider_source":"model","reasoning_source":"provider_field","default_collapsed":true,"agent_id":"main","sequence":96,"stream_source":"live","channel":"provider_thinking:model:provider_field"},{"id":"live_call_b3a28aea08d6_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_b3a28aea08d6","agent_id":"main","sequence":97,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_8b96a8d0f64a_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_8b96a8d0f64a","agent_id":"main","sequence":100,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_a0bd55bf1241_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_a0bd55bf1241","agent_id":"main","sequence":102,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_8f3f1dc61e06_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_8f3f1dc61e06","agent_id":"main","sequence":104,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"part_bdf0932c456a","type":"text","text":"## The colleague-friendly takeaway\n\n**Konza’s clearest signal is intermittent particle bursts at W038, consistent with smoke-bearing air reaching that sensor during the April 15, 2022 burn experiment. Wind was not calm during the largest burst. CO₂ changed too, but it did not track particles proportionally and cannot, by itself, identify combustion emissions.**\n\n### What the measurements show\n\nAll times below are CDT, UTC−05:00. Values are sample-weighted means within each interval.\n\n| April 15 interval | Optical particle estimate, µg/m³ | Horizontal wind, m/s | Dry CO₂, µmol/mol |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 08:00–11:00 | 1.43 | 4.94 | 435.27 |\n| 15:00–16:00 | 3.18 | 6.01 | 449.95 |\n| 16:22–16:23 | 2.60 | 5.76 | 455.25 |\n| 16:41–16:42 | 3,538.36 | 7.35 | 455.10 |\n\nThese comparisons were recomputed from the [aligned minute observations](workspace-file:/D:/CLIO-Develop/ndp-showcases-20261019/konza/konza-aligned-minutes.csv).\n\n- **Particles arrived in bursts.** The largest individual reading was **12,758 µg/m³ at 16:41:42.748**. Lower-level elevation persisted into the evening, rather than everything returning immediately to morning levels. This describes one sensor’s observations, not prairie-wide exposure.\n- **A simple “smoke accumulated because the air was calm” explanation does not fit the wind-speed measurements.** The peak-particle minute averaged 7.35 m/s, versus 6.01 m/s during the preceding 15:00–16:00 comparison hour. That does not rule out turbulence, stability or changing plume position.\n- **CO₂ is not a particle surrogate here.** At 16:22, CO₂ was slightly higher than during the largest particle minute, while particles were only 2.60 µg/m³. Temporal overlap is real, but it is not source attribution or a measured transport lag.\n\n## Explore the figure\n\nThe interactive figure above starts with **April 15, 08:00–20:00**.\n\n1. Set the hour window to **16–17** to compare the low-particle, high-CO₂ period around 16:22 with the particle burst around 16:41.\n2. Choose **April 16**, then **9.75–10.25** hours, to inspect the unresolved CO₂ episode around 10:00.\n3. Hover over observations for counts, ranges, particle-gap duration and wind exclusions.\n\nDots show **minute means**; whiskers show **observed minima and maxima**, not confidence intervals. The particle axis compresses large values using a symlog scale. Coverage strips distinguish missing records, gap-affected minutes, excluded wind samples and suspect CO₂. I reviewed the rendered overview and both close-ups and checked that the controls returned the intended minute counts.\n\n## Where the interpretation stops\n\n- **Particle size and calibration:** ES-642 is an optical mass estimate. The installed size-selective inlet and smoke-specific calibration are unverified, so these are **not confirmed PM2.5 or PM10 measurements**, and an AQI calculation would be unjustified.\n- **Gaps and screening:** The 65-hour particle record contains **12 whole minutes without readings**; the longest inter-reading interval is **73.99 seconds**. Nothing is interpolated. Wind statistics exclude 256 flagged samples, including **128 of 1,200** during the peak minute.\n- **CO₂ validity:** Negative readings occur during **April 16, 10:00–10:06**, and **April 17, 09:30–09:36**, despite nominal diagnostics. They remain visible and flagged, not accepted as ambient concentrations.\n- **Timing and geometry:** Particle timestamps are publication/receipt times; independent clock synchronization, inlet delays, north orientation and sensor geometry are unverified. These records therefore do **not** establish a plume trajectory, combustion-specific enhancement, emission factor or flux. The other inspected wind/gas streams have no April 15 records. See the [sensor and packet audit](workspace-file:/D:/CLIO-Develop/ndp-showcases-20261019/konza/konza-wind-co2-coverage.md).\n\nThe downloadable **`konza-explorer-minutes.csv`** retains the measurements, counts, gaps and review flags behind the figure.","agent_id":"main","sequence":106,"stream_source":"live","channel":"answer"},{"id":"part_c16b870293d9","type":"artifact","artifact_id":"artifact_d660b4bce7ff4909b164db7651895fb9","agent_id":"main","sequence":107}],"run_id":"6dfdf0f1-d306-4446-aabc-81dcfcde0d00","usage":{"input":2491835,"output":11225,"cache_read":2274176,"cache_write":0},"stop_reason":"end_turn","completed_at":"2026-10-19T18:29:15.835273+00:00"},{"id":"42a357b3-2c57-44d4-95ab-a2dcf2db03f3","session_id":"sess_b1bbf58c66a5","role":"user","created_at":"2026-10-19T18:29:18.622664+00:00","blocks":[{"id":"part_bd3094b9fb5e","type":"text","text":"Are the biggest particle episodes also the largest CO₂ episodes, or does the answer depend on the time window? Quantify the comparison without implying that correlation proves a cause.","channel":"answer"}],"run_id":"42a357b3-2c57-44d4-95ab-a2dcf2db03f3","model":{"provider_id":"codex","model_id":"gpt-6.1-sol"},"usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cache_read":0,"cache_write":0}},{"id":"msg_asst_c419ae5a99e6","session_id":"sess_b1bbf58c66a5","role":"assistant","created_at":"2026-10-19T18:33:51.792640+00:00","blocks":[{"id":"live_injection_75ef4ff3dc93","type":"injection","source":"todos","text":"## Todo checklist (your current task list — reconcile before finishing)\n\n[x] Inspect Konza measurements, provenance, gaps and sensor limitations\n[x] Validate key findings and build an exploratory figure with coverage visible\n[x] Review the rendered figure and explain supported conclusions","call_id":"","agent_id":"main","sequence":1},{"id":"live_call_5ad539a94b41_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_5ad539a94b41","agent_id":"main","sequence":2,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"part_86e61ef407c7","type":"text","text":"I’ll compare paired one-minute averages on April 15 across several time windows, then check whether averaging into 15-minute bins changes the result. I’ll also compare the highest-ranked minutes directly, since correlation alone does not tell us whether the biggest episodes coincide.","agent_id":"main","sequence":4,"stream_source":"live","channel":"next_thought"},{"id":"live_call_5bbb0b32da7d_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_5bbb0b32da7d","agent_id":"main","sequence":5,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_24d277c858a2_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_24d277c858a2","agent_id":"main","sequence":6,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_6a9ead6c7015_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_6a9ead6c7015","agent_id":"main","sequence":9,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"part_d2dbfc3f537d","type":"reasoning","started_at":"2026-10-19T18:30:04.141441+00:00","completed_at":"2026-10-19T18:30:15.728338+00:00","duration_ms":11586.891199927777,"text":"**Evaluating data aggregation**\n\nI’m considering how to manage window boundaries and time indexing for gas consumption data. I need to aggregate each available count independently across all minute data while ensuring I don’t pair bins unless specific conditions are met. I have to check for missing data and calculate the total available bins accurately. Using simple metrics like the Pearson and Spearman correlation could be helpful, but I need to focus on maintaining robustness with my time window choices.**Calculating differences for correlation**\n\nI need to calculate the first differences across adjacent one-minute intervals within each window. This will help me analyze the correlation trend effectively, using both the Pearson correlation and rank correlation. For example, I want to see how a full Pearson correlation of 0.21 and a rank correlation of 0.69 compare. I suspect there will be a strong contrast in the results, so it’s essential to see how the primary full one-hour differences play out. Let’s dig into those calculations!","source":"provider","provider_source":"model","reasoning_source":"provider_field","default_collapsed":true,"agent_id":"main","sequence":11,"stream_source":"live","channel":"provider_thinking:model:provider_field"},{"id":"live_call_aecfad46776a_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_aecfad46776a","agent_id":"main","sequence":12,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"part_91c5870ff515","type":"reasoning","started_at":"2026-10-19T18:30:29.904082+00:00","completed_at":"2026-10-19T18:30:44.183011+00:00","duration_ms":14278.923799982294,"text":"**Analyzing user definitions**\n\nI want to clarify the user’s definition of \"episodes.\" It seems they refer to significant events and that the \"biggest minute averages\" might not reflect independent fire events. I need to quantify the block maxima over 15 minutes for different metrics, particularly CO2 levels. I'll compute the largest values during specified time windows. My goal is to generate a report with the top pairs and a noon episode, ensuring the CSV file is clear with added ranks and period categories for better analysis.**Summarizing file requirements**\n\nI’m considering that 958 columns might be appropriate for my summary artifact, either in JSON or CSV format, including entries for average 1 and 15. I may need to create two files plus a report in markdown, though that might not be necessary. I've saved the stats in JSON, but they aren't deliverable yet. The final citation for the CSV artifact should reference the actual source, but the instructions indicate no markdown links are allowed, which is a bit confusing. I want to ensure everything aligns for effective analysis.","source":"provider","provider_source":"model","reasoning_source":"provider_field","default_collapsed":true,"agent_id":"main","sequence":14,"stream_source":"live","channel":"provider_thinking:model:provider_field"},{"id":"live_call_c44365b5272a_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_c44365b5272a","agent_id":"main","sequence":15,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_4fef464b7fc0_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_4fef464b7fc0","agent_id":"main","sequence":16,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_163d13cee502_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_163d13cee502","agent_id":"main","sequence":17,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_be9549ff2853_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_be9549ff2853","agent_id":"main","sequence":21,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_1fe0885b1977_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_1fe0885b1977","agent_id":"main","sequence":22,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_f3d9ba0ff378_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_f3d9ba0ff378","agent_id":"main","sequence":24,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_e12d25bf3c37_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_e12d25bf3c37","agent_id":"main","sequence":27,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_6f50b8e6fd21_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_6f50b8e6fd21","agent_id":"main","sequence":29,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_e60e2752668c_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_e60e2752668c","agent_id":"main","sequence":31,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_a2ui_b7b39c276d7d","type":"a2ui","surface_id":"konza-particle-co2-association","sequence":32},{"id":"live_call_b184a95d8f71_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_b184a95d8f71","agent_id":"main","sequence":34,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_59917add6f76_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_59917add6f76","agent_id":"main","sequence":36,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_01468cc11960_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_01468cc11960","agent_id":"main","sequence":38,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_9cdf78361bfd_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_9cdf78361bfd","agent_id":"main","sequence":40,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_6f051e7c7ed0_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_6f051e7c7ed0","agent_id":"main","sequence":43,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"live_call_4fb7f4746f96_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_4fb7f4746f96","agent_id":"main","sequence":45,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"part_c627af01ccd2","type":"reasoning","started_at":"2026-10-19T18:32:50.218644+00:00","completed_at":"2026-10-19T18:32:59.412586+00:00","duration_ms":9193.938500015065,"text":"**Analyzing CO2 Data Methods**\n\nI'm feeling confident about avoiding unnecessary filters since the tools aren't available. For the CO2 variables, I've noticed a late evening drop to 420, which excludes irregular days. I'm not attributing uncertainty to biological trends. For analysis, I could provide Spearman's rank correlation, showing an agreement in ranking rather than amplitudes, with a correlation of 0.8 and a coefficient at 0.22. \n\nI’m considering presenting final insights in about 650 words, including tables for ranking and correlation.**Smoothing and Data Analysis Approach**\n\nI’m considering adding smoothing factors of 1, 15, and 60 minutes, which increases the rise from 0.20 to 0.32 and then to 0.49. I have a significant data set of n=95,864. However, I'm unsure about spatial information affecting lag, particularly with an unknown clock shift. \n\nI want to keep user needs in mind, ensuring a succinct comparison of the largest particles at specific times without overwhelming them with too many data points. I'll also mention correlation and smoothing for clarity in the final report, retaining computational details for accuracy.**Finalizing Data Report**\n\nI'm finalizing the citations for the original sources that support the derived numbers, and I need to include a note about calculating these from the aligned minute file. I'll be sure to cite the summary CSV with the artifact name. However, I can't use names in the report.\n\nI need to ensure the summary counts and rankings are accurate using numpy, since it's dependable. The rankings implementation using an average formula seems correct, where starting at 0 and ending at 1 gives me a value of 1.","source":"provider","provider_source":"model","reasoning_source":"provider_field","default_collapsed":true,"agent_id":"main","sequence":47,"stream_source":"live","channel":"provider_thinking:model:provider_field"},{"id":"live_call_a25a83877a98_call","type":"tool","tool_id":"call_a25a83877a98","agent_id":"main","sequence":48,"stream_source":"live"},{"id":"part_af74e7c6d458","type":"text","text":"**Only partly, and the strength of the relationship depends substantially on the time window and averaging interval.** The main particle burst overlaps a CO₂ high, but the two signals do not have matching rankings or proportional amplitudes.\n\nI compared W038 particle estimates with LI-7200 dry CO₂ using the [aligned minute observations](workspace-file:/D:/CLIO-Develop/ndp-showcases-20261019/konza/konza-aligned-minutes.csv) for **April 15, 2022**. All times below are CDT.\n\n## Do the largest readings coincide?\n\nAmong the **958 paired minutes from 08:00 to midnight**, only **4 of the 10 highest particle minutes** were also among the **10 highest CO₂ minutes**.\n\n| Minute beginning | Particles, µg/m³ | Particle rank | Dry CO₂, µmol/mol | CO₂ rank |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 16:41 | 3,538.36 | 1 | 455.10 | 8 |\n| 16:43 | 1,712.38 | 3 | 455.79 | 1 |\n| 16:23 | 3.52 | 505 | 455.26 | 5 |\n| 18:32 | 864.76 | 5 | 451.83 | 120 |\n\nRank 1 means largest within that paired-minute set.\n\nThe **16:23 counterexample is especially useful**: CO₂ was its fifth-highest minute value, while particles were below the median of the paired minutes. Conversely, the fifth-largest particle minute at 18:32 was not among the most extreme CO₂ minutes.\n\nSeveral top-ranked minutes belong to the **same continuous burst**. These counts describe overlap between minutes, not ten independent plume events. The two-minute separation between the individual maxima is also **not a validated transport lag**.\n\n## How much does the window matter?\n\nThese correlations give every available paired minute equal weight.\n\n| April 15 window | Paired minutes | Pearson r | Spearman ρ |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 08:00–24:00 | 958 | **0.20** | **0.30** |\n| 08:00–20:00 | 718 | 0.22 | **0.80** |\n| 12:00–14:00 | 119 | −0.03 | −0.12 |\n| 15:00–16:00 | 60 | −0.52 | −0.62 |\n| 16:00–17:00 | 60 | **0.44** | **0.58** |\n| 16:30–17:00 | 30 | 0.52 | 0.31 |\n\n- **Pearson r** measures linear association in the untransformed concentrations and is sensitive to large bursts.\n- **Spearman ρ** measures agreement in rankings, using average ranks for ties. It does not require proportional changes.\n\nThe distinction matters: through 20:00, particle and CO₂ rankings broadly rise together, giving ρ = 0.80, yet their amplitudes remain poorly proportional, with r = 0.22. Including the later evening reduces rank agreement to 0.30. Even adjacent afternoon windows have different signs.\n\n**Averaging changes the answer too.** Across 08:00–24:00, Pearson r rises from **0.20 for one-minute means** to **0.32 for clock-aligned 15-minute means**, and **0.49 for hourly means**. Those coarser comparisons contain 64 and 16 paired blocks, respectively. Means within each block are sample-weighted. Smoothing emphasizes broader changes while suppressing short bursts; it does not establish a stronger physical coupling.\n\n## How to interpret this safely\n\nThe new scatter figure above colors minutes by time of day and labels the key counterexamples. Hover for concentrations, ranks and gap flags.\n\nTwo empty particle minutes were omitted without interpolation. Removing another **32 partially gap-affected minutes** barely changes full-window Pearson r: **0.196 → 0.195**. I restricted this comparison to April 15 rather than mixing in the unresolved negative CO₂ periods on later days.\n\nThese are **descriptive associations**, not causal tests. The windows overlap, adjacent minutes are serially dependent, and broad time trends can create correlation. Unverified clock synchronization, inlet delays, sensor geometry and calibration further limit interpretation. No background subtraction or lag optimization was applied.\n\n**Bottom line:** the largest afternoon particle burst and a CO₂ high overlap, but “the biggest particle episodes are also the biggest CO₂ episodes” is not a reliable general rule.\n\nThe downloadable **`konza-particle-co2-pairs.csv`** and **`konza-window-correlations.csv`** retain the ranked observations and window calculations.","agent_id":"main","sequence":50,"stream_source":"live","channel":"answer"},{"id":"part_f2857108771c","type":"artifact","artifact_id":"artifact_db884b6c5bd2499e8d181b519bb6a428","agent_id":"main","sequence":51},{"id":"part_ce7dc051aaa7","type":"artifact","artifact_id":"artifact_bf0c121d16c44e02b13fb75996128048","agent_id":"main","sequence":52}],"run_id":"42a357b3-2c57-44d4-95ab-a2dcf2db03f3","usage":{"input":2425623,"output":10465,"cache_read":2248960,"cache_write":0},"stop_reason":"end_turn","completed_at":"2026-10-19T18:33:51.792649+00:00"},{"id":"a2120cfd-