dependencies","label":"","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"uri":"","command":"","timed_out":false,"status":"","detail":"","items":[],"action_label":""},{"id":"os","type":"text","media_type":"","text":"Os: Windows","label":"","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"uri":"","command":"","timed_out":false,"status":"","detail":"","items":[],"action_label":""},{"id":"architecture","type":"text","media_type":"","text":"Architecture: AMD64","label":"","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"uri":"","command":"","timed_out":false,"status":"","detail":"","items":[],"action_label":""},{"id":"python_version","type":"text","media_type":"","text":"Python version: 3.14.8","label":"","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"uri":"","command":"","timed_out":false,"status":"","detail":"","items":[],"action_label":""},{"id":"node_version","type":"text","media_type":"","text":"Node version: v24.19.0","label":"","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"uri":"","command":"","timed_out":false,"status":"","detail":"","items":[],"action_label":""}]},"duration_ms":2641.7226791381836,"started_at":"2026-10-19T18:24:14.362352+00:00","completed_at":"2026-10-19T18:24:17.004075+00:00"},{"id":"call_b058b93de822","session_id":"sess_b1bbf58c66a5","run_id":"6dfdf0f1-d306-4446-aabc-81dcfcde0d00","name":"fs_read_file","title":"Read File","state":"succeeded","input":{"_clio_task_description":"Read Konza analysis methods and particle findings","access_reason":"","filepath":"D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza\\konza-burn-analysis.md"},"output":{"content":[],"structuredContent":{"content":"# Konza burn observations and what they support\r\n\r\n## Colleague summary\r\nOn April 15, 2022, the W038 ES-642 recorded large, intermittent particle bursts, with lower-level elevation persisting into the evening. This is consistent with smoke-bearing air intermittently reaching W038 during the burn experiment. It is not proof of ignition time, the cause of every burst, prairie-wide exposure or a health-standard exceedance.\r\n\r\nThe previously unread large files have now been downloaded and scanned in full. Both CSAT3 wind and LI-7200 CO2 cover the burn day. Their observations provide context, but do not establish a plume trajectory, a calm-air accumulation mechanism, a combustion-specific CO2 enhancement, an emission factor or an eddy-covariance flux.\r\n\r\nAll local times use America/Chicago, CDT (UTC-05:00). Every interval is start-inclusive and end-exclusive. Reported averages are sample-weighted, not gap-adjusted exposure estimates.\r\n\r\n## Key measured figures\r\n| April 15 CDT interval | Particle mean (µg/m³) | LI-7200 dry CO2 mean (µmol/mol) | Screened CSAT3 horizontal speed mean (m/s) |\r\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\r\n| 08:00–11:00 | 1.43 | 435.27 | 4.94 |\r\n| 12:19–12:20 | 764.85 | 443.87 | 7.10 |\r\n| 15:00–16:00 | 3.18 | 449.95 | 6.01 |\r\n| 16:30–17:00 | 450.85 | 454.18 | 6.25 |\r\n| 16:41–16:42 | 3,538.36 | 455.10 | 7.35 |\r\n| 18:32–18:33 | 864.76 | 451.83 | 7.25 |\r\n| 19:00–20:00 | 7.36 | 445.91 | 5.55 |\r\n\r\nThe largest individual particle reading was **12,758 µg/m³ at 16:41:42.748 CDT**. The 16:41 minute contains 55 particle readings, 1,200 LI-7200 Data packets and 1,200 CSAT3 records; 128 flagged wind records were excluded, leaving 1,072 for its speed statistic. Min–max ranges in the views are observed variability, not confidence intervals.\r\n\r\n## What we can conclude\r\n- The particle change was episodic, not a smooth all-day rise. April 15 has 51,918 recorded readings from 08:00 to midnight: 199 (0.38%) exceed 1,000 µg/m³ and 2,573 (4.96%) exceed 100 µg/m³. These are descriptive sample fractions, not health thresholds or fractions of elapsed time.\r\n- Lower-level elevation also lingered. The 16:00–19:00 mean/median was 132.69/29 µg/m³; 19:00–24:00 was 7.73/6, compared with 1.43/1 at 08:00–11:00. Block summaries do not prove a continuously elevated plateau through unobserved intervals.\r\n- CO2 shows a modest local rise near the main particle burst. Its largest April 15 minute mean was 455.79 µmol/mol at 16:43, compared with 453.21 at 16:35. This is descriptive temporal concurrence, not a lag estimate or source attribution. A near-equal CO2 mean of 455.25 occurred at 16:22 while particles averaged only 2.60 µg/m³, so the two signals are not simply proportional.\r\n- The screened horizontal wind-speed mean at the particle-peak minute was 7.35 m/s, versus 6.01 over 15:00–16:00. These observations do not support a simple calm-air explanation. They do not rule out effects of turbulence, stability, source motion or spatially varying dispersion.\r\n\r\n## What we cannot conclude\r\n- ES-642 is an optical particle-mass estimate, not chemistry, CO or CO2. The installed size-selective inlet is undocumented; do not call this confirmed PM2.5/PM10 or calculate AQI. Smoke-specific calibration and full instrument-status screening are unavailable. Sensitivity is 1 µg/m³, so precise morning-to-peak ratios are misleading.\r\n- Raw BME680 resistance is gas-sensitive electrical resistance affected by gases and humidity, not a specific gas concentration or particulate measure.\r\n- Sensor geometry, north orientation, inlet/tubing delay, calibration operations and independently synchronized clocks have not been established. Raw CSAT3 components are not a defensible north-referenced plume trajectory. No arbitrary lag shift or background subtraction was applied.\r\n- CO2 is not combustion-specific; slow changes, biological exchange and instrument operations are alternatives. Coincidence alone cannot isolate burn emissions. No emission factor, regional exposure or flux is calculated.\r\n\r\n## Coverage and quality caveats\r\nThe particle series contains 212,045 readings over 65 hours, April 15 08:00 to April 18 00:59 CDT, approximately 1 Hz. Twelve whole minutes contain no readings; the longest inter-reading interval is 73.991 seconds. No interpolation fills those gaps. Nominal reported flow is 2.0 L/min; status flags for the particle channel are unavailable. Timestamps are publication/receipt timestamps, not independently verified acquisition times.\r\n\r\nCSAT3 contains 4,680,001 records. LI-7200 contains 4,679,516 archive records holding 4,679,998 unique measurement packets, at approximately 20 Hz from just before April 15 07:00 to April 17 23:59:59 CDT. The largest CSAT3 inter-record gap is 0.051 s; the largest LI-7200 measurement-packet gap is 0.100 s (the archive-record gap is 0.150 s), with no backward transitions or adjacent duplicate timestamps. All 256 flagged CSAT3 samples occur on April 15 and were excluded from wind statistics. This is basic diagnostic screening, not complete meteorological quality assurance.\r\n\r\nLI-7200 `CO2MFd` is dry mole fraction in µmol/mol; `CO2D` is density in mmol/m³ and was not substituted for it. `DiagVal=8191` throughout is not an error code: bits 4–12 are set to their OK states; bits 0–3 encode signal strength. `DiagVal2=0` throughout. Nominal diagnostics do not establish valid ambient sampling: negative dry CO2 values occur in the calendar-minute bins in April 16 10:00–10:06 and April 17 09:30–09:36 CDT. These are unresolved, potentially operational/calibration-related intervals, not accepted ambient concentrations. They are retained and flagged in the aligned export, not silently removed. None falls on April 15. Do not generalize April 15 results to unscreened later periods.\r\n\r\nThe other inspected files contain no April 15 records: WindObserver II begins April 16 08:10; Picarro G2131-i begins April 16 09:38 with nonempty buffers around 10:02; LI-840A has one isolated April 14 record and then resumes April 16 09:24, a roughly 50-hour gap. Picarro field positions remain unmapped here. Their absence is neither corroboration of nor evidence against a smoke interpretation.\r\n\r\n## Corrected time-window semantics and controls\r\nThe explorer now selects minute-bucket midpoints with the server's inclusive range filter. For supported 15-minute-aligned endpoints this exactly implements start-inclusive/end-exclusive windows. **16:30–17:00 selects 30 buckets, 16:30 through 16:59, totaling 1,590 particle readings.** The adjacent 17:00–17:30 window selects 30 different buckets and 1,657 readings; there is no duplicated 17:00 bucket.\r\n\r\nAll 19,012 supported date/window combinations were checked numerically, including full days, empty windows and midnight boundaries. A calendar day has 1,440 grid buckets, not 1,440 observed minutes. Client inspection also returned 30 rows in both panels for the corrected half-hour and its adjacent half-hour; April 16 full-day returned 1,440. Matching client captures were reviewed for the corrected half-hour, April 16 full day and fixed peak close-up. The adjacent-window capture was unavailable when its viewer became hidden, although its resolved query and 30-row result were inspected. The peak view remains explicitly fixed to April 15, independent of date/hour controls. Dot separators are absent from titles/captions. Tooltip clicking and linked selection were not exercised.\r\n\r\n## Provenance and reproducibility\r\nSource collection: NDP dataset `335fa1cb-d6d2-4e96-bc8e-c309d957b24d`, **NEON MDP / Sage / WIFIRE BP3D: Konza Prairie Burn Experiment**, publisher sage-neon. This is distinct from the separate classification-results dataset.\r\n\r\n| File | NDP resource ID | Interpretation used here |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| 21-W038.ndjson | 4e1ccb5d-aef9-4c06-846a-e45eb6d72fd1 | W038 `env.air_quality.conc`, `meta.sensor=es642`; mg/m³ multiplied by 1,000 |\r\n| 1-reading.sensor.csat3.pkl | 07a135d1-bcd3-4e12-aff5-1e91d667fe5e | MDP5 source 46586, readout_time, ux/uy/uz components and flags b12–b15 |\r\n| 7-reading.sensor.li7200_raw.pkl | 8a6fc572-d84b-4c2f-9f92-e141d7e99687 | MDP5 source 001c94040b1f, each Ethernet Data packet Date/Time, CO2MFd and diagnostics |\r\n| 17-reading.sensor.windobserverii.pkl | ac7e07d8-a67c-4025-a5bf-3ccf9c324d4b | Coverage and basic status only |\r\n| 8-reading.sensor.li840a.pkl | db35cec6-e8b1-4543-b203-b301fdc99a14 | Coverage only, no burn-day records |\r\n| 2-reading.sensor.g2131i_raw.pkl | 90d20433-16ca-48ed-a44f-dbebce72f473 | Coverage and buffer presence only |\r\n\r\nAll original snapshots remain read-only. Particle snapshot: `source_4343dbad7989498b90706b7ce7c631e3 / snapshot_b0b3531b79f8464e8b4dcba08a290c7e`. Lower-frequency snapshot: `source_99ddabb797e54d9ab478feb450c82cf9 / snapshot_2fba88b9720c450cb07e2384f0c0d1fc`. High-frequency snapshot: `source_6ee565089fc9473e94f395f2d2a3d1ca / snapshot_b51fc33834954da38ad898e7b30ecba0`.\r\n\r\nPickle streams were decoded with a primitive-only pickletools interpreter that rejects executable/global/reduction opcodes, not unrestricted pickle loading. Streaming scans aggregate count, mean, population standard deviation, minimum and maximum into calendar minutes. Nonfinite and -9999 numeric fields are excluded; all four CSAT3 diagnostic bits must be false. Horizontal speed is the sample mean of sqrt(ux²+uy²), not the magnitude of a block-mean vector. CO2 diagnostics are retained, not treated as zero-is-good sonic flags. Negative CO2 values remain visibly flagged for review.\r\n\r\n## Supporting files and references\r\n- `konza-aligned-minutes.csv`: complete April 15–18 calendar-minute grid, particle means/ranges/counts/gap status, wind record/flag counts and component/speed statistics, dry CO2 means/ranges/counts and diagnostic fields. No interpolation or clock shifting. Wind component/speed units are m/s; CO2MFd units are µmol/mol. Missing high-frequency values after April 17 are not zeros.\r\n- `konza-time-of-day.csv`: corrected explorer grid and midpoint filter key. `konza-particle-pattern.csv`: minute summaries plus a separately identified raw peak window; do not average mixed grains together. `konza-sampling-gaps.csv`: exact adjacent particle timestamp intervals longer than 3 seconds.\r\n- `konza-cross-sensor-coverage.csv`: six streams in ten-minute bins; record presence is not quality approval. `konza-provenance.json`: source and derivative SHA-256 hashes, source profiles and boundary regression results.\r\n- Working programs are retained under `.tmp/clio-documents/`: primitive_pickle.py, scan_high_frequency.py (wind path), scan_li7200_packets.py, analyze_aligned.py, fix_boundary.py and build_provenance.py. The scans retain full-record minute summaries and profiles there.\r\n\r\nManufacturer and method sources:\r\n1. [ES-642 datasheet, pp. 1–2](https://metone.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/02/ES-642-datasheet.pdf), optical measurement, inlet options and sensitivity.\r\n2. [Contemporaneous Sage plugin source](https://github.com/waggle-sensor/plugin-air-quality/blob/85910d2d187d0bbdb5a93c6d9aac28e6d46e940e/main.py), concentration pass-through and published fields; a contemporaneous source, not a verified container digest match.\r\n3. [Bosch BME680 datasheet, especially §4.2](https://www.bosch-sensortec.com/media/boschsensortec/downloads/datasheets/bst-bme680-ds001.pdf), resistance response and measurement limitations.\r\n4. [LI-7200/RS manufacturer manual](https://licor.app.boxenterprise.net/shared/static/k8b90wiwdhietarx04qd), physical PDF p. 159 (§8-11) for DiagVal and p. 288 (E-10) for CO2MFd/CO2D mapping; [diagnostic conventions](https://www.licor.com/support/LI-7200RS/topics/diagnostics-eddypro.html), including CSAT3 bits 12–15. The manual defines channels, not this deployment's calibration history.\r\n5. [NEON wind ATBD revision B](https://data.neonscience.org/api/v0/documents/NEON.DOC.000780vB), raw components and orientation requirements.\r\n6. [Experiment exporter notebook](https://github.com/sagecontinuum/neon-kafka-exporter/blob/main/burn-event/post-processing/exploratory-data-analysis.ipynb), contextual example only; actual coverage above comes from downloaded bytes, not notebook figures.\r\n\r\n## LI-7200 packet-count audit\r\nThe initial archive-row pass is superseded. Some archive records contain multiple complete `(Data ...)` packets. The first pass collapsed repeated field names into one dictionary, keeping the last packet's values but using the archive row's readout_time. Thus its CO2 counts were archive-record counts, not measurement-packet counts, and some concentrations could be assigned an earlier packet's time.\r\n\r\nThe replacement scanner parses each balanced Data packet independently, retaining that packet's Date, Time, Ndx, values and diagnostics. Measurements are binned by their own embedded Date/Time rather than one timestamp for the containing archive record. Both observations are used; a packet is not dropped simply because it shares an archive record. Counts of archive records, all packets and unique measurement timestamps are reported separately. The immutable raw archive remains available for packet-level reconstruction; the minute derivative is an aggregate, not a lossless raw-data substitute. Embedded times are interpreted as UTC consistently with their matching readout_time; independent cross-instrument clock synchronization remains unverified.\r\n\r\nExample from archive record 915: outer readout_time is 2022-04-15 12:00:45.600 UTC. It contains Ndx 180690197 at 12:00:45.600 with CO2MFd 426.514 µmol/mol, and Ndx 180690212 at 12:00:45.650 with CO2MFd 426.502. The earlier pass kept 426.502 at .600 and lost 426.514. The corrected pass keeps both at their respective times, 50 ms apart. This illustrates why archive-row completeness cannot establish a measurement-time gap or sample count.\r\n\r\nThe full scan found 4,679,034 single-packet records and 482 double-packet records, totaling 4,679,998 packets and 4,679,998 unique measurement times. There were no missing packet Date/Time or CO2MFd fields, duplicate measurement timestamps or backward packet-time transitions. Of the extra packets, 480 were 50 ms later than the containing record readout_time and 2 were 100 ms later. All packets were included; no deduplication was needed. Counts changed in 458 minute bins. The 16:41 minute increases from 1,199 archive rows to 1,200 packets; its mean changes from 455.097306 to 455.097548 µmol/mol. The key table values are unchanged at two decimals. The maximum absolute minute-mean correction anywhere is 0.246949 µmol/mol, in the unresolved April 17 09:30 CDT interval. This correction does not resolve calibration or cross-instrument timing uncertainty.\r\n\r\n\nThe aligned export explicitly separates `co2_packets` / `CO2MFd_n` (embedded measurement time) from `co2_archive_records_by_readout_time` (outer archive timestamp). The packet-count CSV compares both definitions for every covered minute. April 15 minute-mean corrections are at most 0.000769 µmol/mol; the rounded key figures and qualitative interpretation are unchanged. Particle gaps and the half-open explorer boundary correction are unaffected by this CO2 parser fix.\r\n\n## Updated context-view review\nThe corrected three-panel particle/CO2/wind comparison and six-stream coverage were rendered locally and visually inspected at approximately 740–770 pixels wide. The comparison retains the low-particle/high-CO2 counterexample around 16:22; it does not select only the matching peak. The shared time axes, symlog particle scale, linear gas/wind scales and observed min–max ranges were checked. Client inspection resolved the corrected comparison to 60 rows and coverage to 3,240 rows. A whole-client capture timed out; component capture then found a hidden/busy viewer. The inspected viewer had a very narrow 183-pixel width. Therefore the revised context view is not claimed client-pixel-verified or readable at that narrow size; expand it for plotting. This limitation does not apply to the separately captured and reviewed corrected particle-window explorer.\r\n","path":"D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza\\konza-burn-analysis.md","size_bytes":16086,"truncated":false}},"presentation":{"action":"Read","subject":"file-link","summary":"16086 bytes","blocks":[{"id":"file-link","type":"link","media_type":"","text":"","label":"konza-burn-analysis.md","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"target":"file","uri":"D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza\\konza-burn-analysis.md","command":"","timed_out":false,"status":"","detail":"","items":[],"action_label":""},{"id":"file","type":"markdown","media_type":"","text":"# Konza burn observations and what they support\r\n\r\n## Colleague summary\r\nOn April 15, 2022, the W038 ES-642 recorded large, intermittent particle bursts, with lower-level elevation persisting into the evening. This is consistent with smoke-bearing air intermittently reaching W038 during the burn experiment. It is not proof of ignition time, the cause of every burst, prairie-wide exposure or a health-standard exceedance.\r\n\r\nThe previously unread large files have now been downloaded and scanned in full. Both CSAT3 wind and LI-7200 CO2 cover the burn day. Their observations provide context, but do not establish a plume trajectory, a calm-air accumulation mechanism, a combustion-specific CO2 enhancement, an emission factor or an eddy-covariance flux.\r\n\r\nAll local times use America/Chicago, CDT (UTC-05:00). Every interval is start-inclusive and end-exclusive. Reported averages are sample-weighted, not gap-adjusted exposure estimates.\r\n\r\n## Key measured figures\r\n| April 15 CDT interval | Particle mean (µg/m³) | LI-7200 dry CO2 mean (µmol/mol) | Screened CSAT3 horizontal speed mean (m/s) |\r\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\r\n| 08:00–11:00 | 1.43 | 435.27 | 4.94 |\r\n| 12:19–12:20 | 764.85 | 443.87 | 7.10 |\r\n| 15:00–16:00 | 3.18 | 449.95 | 6.01 |\r\n| 16:30–17:00 | 450.85 | 454.18 | 6.25 |\r\n| 16:41–16:42 | 3,538.36 | 455.10 | 7.35 |\r\n| 18:32–18:33 | 864.76 | 451.83 | 7.25 |\r\n| 19:00–20:00 | 7.36 | 445.91 | 5.55 |\r\n\r\nThe largest individual particle reading was **12,758 µg/m³ at 16:41:42.748 CDT**. The 16:41 minute contains 55 particle readings, 1,200 LI-7200 Data packets and 1,200 CSAT3 records; 128 flagged wind records were excluded, leaving 1,072 for its speed statistic. Min–max ranges in the views are observed variability, not confidence intervals.\r\n\r\n## What we can conclude\r\n- The particle change was episodic, not a smooth all-day rise. April 15 has 51,918 recorded readings from 08:00 to midnight: 199 (0.38%) exceed 1,000 µg/m³ and 2,573 (4.96%) exceed 100 µg/m³. These are descriptive sample fractions, not health thresholds or fractions of elapsed time.\r\n- Lower-level elevation also lingered. The 16:00–19:00 mean/median was 132.69/29 µg/m³; 19:00–24:00 was 7.73/6, compared with 1.43/1 at 08:00–11:00. Block summaries do not prove a continuously elevated plateau through unobserved intervals.\r\n- CO2 shows a modest local rise near the main particle burst. Its largest April 15 minute mean was 455.79 µmol/mol at 16:43, compared with 453.21 at 16:35. This is descriptive temporal concurrence, not a lag estimate or source attribution. A near-equal CO2 mean of 455.25 occurred at 16:22 while particles averaged only 2.60 µg/m³, so the two signals are not simply proportional.\r\n- The screened horizontal wind-speed mean at the particle-peak minute was 7.35 m/s, versus 6.01 over 15:00–16:00. These observations do not support a simple calm-air explanation. They do not rule out effects of turbulence, stability, source motion or spatially varying dispersion.\r\n\r\n## What we cannot conclude\r\n- ES-642 is an optical particle-mass estimate, not chemistry, CO or CO2. The installed size-selective inlet is undocumented; do not call this confirmed PM2.5/PM10 or calculate AQI. Smoke-specific calibration and full instrument-status screening are unavailable. Sensitivity is 1 µg/m³, so precise morning-to-peak ratios are misleading.\r\n- Raw BME680 resistance is gas-sensitive electrical resistance affected by gases and humidity, not a specific gas concentration or particulate measure.\r\n- Sensor geometry, north orientation, inlet/tubing delay, calibration operations and independently synchronized clocks have not been established. Raw CSAT3 components are not a defensible north-referenced plume trajectory. No arbitrary lag shift or background subtraction was applied.\r\n- CO2 is not combustion-specific; slow changes, biological exchange and instrument operations are alternatives. Coincidence alone cannot isolate burn emissions. No emission factor, regional exposure or flux is calculated.\r\n\r\n## Coverage and quality caveats\r\nThe particle series contains 212,045 readings over 65 hours, April 15 08:00 to April 18 00:59 CDT, approximately 1 Hz. Twelve whole minutes contain no readings; the longest inter-reading interval is 73.991 seconds. No interpolation fills those gaps. Nominal reported flow is 2.0 L/min; status flags for the particle channel are unavailable. Timestamps are publication/receipt timestamps, not independently verified acquisition times.\r\n\r\nCSAT3 contains 4,680,001 records. LI-7200 contains 4,679,516 archive records holding 4,679,998 unique measurement packets, at approximately 20 Hz from just before April 15 07:00 to April 17 23:59:59 CDT. The largest CSAT3 inter-record gap is 0.051 s; the largest LI-7200 measurement-packet gap is 0.100 s (the archive-record gap is 0.150 s), with no backward transitions or adjacent duplicate timestamps. All 256 flagged CSAT3 samples occur on April 15 and were excluded from wind statistics. This is basic diagnostic screening, not complete meteorological quality assurance.\r\n\r\nLI-7200 `CO2MFd` is dry mole fraction in µmol/mol; `CO2D` is density in mmol/m³ and was not substituted for it. `DiagVal=8191` throughout is not an error code: bits 4–12 are set to their OK states; bits 0–3 encode signal strength. `DiagVal2=0` throughout. Nominal diagnostics do not establish valid ambient sampling: negative dry CO2 values occur in the calendar-minute bins in April 16 10:00–10:06 and April 17 09:30–09:36 CDT. These are unresolved, potentially operational/calibration-related intervals, not accepted ambient concentrations. They are retained and flagged in the aligned export, not silently removed. None falls on April 15. Do not generalize April 15 results to unscreened later periods.\r\n\r\nThe other inspected files contain no April 15 records: WindObserver II begins April 16 08:10; Picarro G2131-i begins April 16 09:38 with nonempty buffers around 10:02; LI-840A has one isolated April 14 record and then resumes April 16 09:24, a roughly 50-hour gap. Picarro field positions remain unmapped here. Their absence is neither corroboration of nor evidence against a smoke interpretation.\r\n\r\n## Corrected time-window semantics and controls\r\nThe explorer now selects minute-bucket midpoints with the server's inclusive range filter. For supported 15-minute-aligned endpoints this exactly implements start-inclusive/end-exclusive windows. **16:30–17:00 selects 30 buckets, 16:30 through 16:59, totaling 1,590 particle readings.** The adjacent 17:00–17:30 window selects 30 different buckets and 1,657 readings; there is no duplicated 17:00 bucket.\r\n\r\nAll 19,012 supported date/window combinations were checked numerically, including full days, empty windows and midnight boundaries. A calendar day has 1,440 grid buckets, not 1,440 observed minutes. Client inspection also returned 30 rows in both panels for the corrected half-hour and its adjacent half-hour; April 16 full-day returned 1,440. Matching client captures were reviewed for the corrected half-hour, April 16 full day and fixed peak close-up. The adjacent-window capture was unavailable when its viewer became hidden, although its resolved query and 30-row result were inspected. The peak view remains explicitly fixed to April 15, independent of date/hour controls. Dot separators are absent from titles/captions. Tooltip clicking and linked selection were not exercised.\r\n\r\n## Provenance and reproducibility\r\nSource collection: NDP dataset `335fa1cb-d6d2-4e96-bc8e-c309d957b24d`, **NEON MDP / Sage / WIFIRE BP3D: Konza Prairie Burn Experiment**, publisher sage-neon. This is distinct from the separate classification-results dataset.\r\n\r\n| File | NDP resource ID | Interpretation used here |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| 21-W038.ndjson | 4e1ccb5d-aef9-4c06-846a-e45eb6d72fd1 | W038 `env.air_quality.conc`, `meta.sensor=es642`; mg/m³ multiplied by 1,000 |\r\n| 1-reading.sensor.csat3.pkl | 07a135d1-bcd3-4e12-aff5-1e91d667fe5e | MDP5 source 46586, readout_time, ux/uy/uz components and flags b12–b15 |\r\n| 7-reading.sensor.li7200_raw.pkl | 8a6fc572-d84b-4c2f-9f92-e141d7e99687 | MDP5 source 001c94040b1f, each Ethernet Data packet Date/Time, CO2MFd and diagnostics |\r\n| 17-reading.sensor.windobserverii.pkl | ac7e07d8-a67c-4025-a5bf-3ccf9c324d4b | Coverage and basic status only |\r\n| 8-reading.sensor.li840a.pkl | db35cec6-e8b1-4543-b203-b301fdc99a14 | Coverage only, no burn-day records |\r\n| 2-reading.sensor.g2131i_raw.pkl | 90d20433-16ca-48ed-a44f-dbebce72f473 | Coverage and buffer presence only |\r\n\r\nAll original snapshots remain read-only. Particle snapshot: `source_4343dbad7989498b90706b7ce7c631e3 / snapshot_b0b3531b79f8464e8b4dcba08a290c7e`. Lower-frequency snapshot: `source_99ddabb797e54d9ab478feb450c82cf9 / snapshot_2fba88b9720c450cb07e2384f0c0d1fc`. High-frequency snapshot: `source_6ee565089fc9473e94f395f2d2a3d1ca / snapshot_b51fc33834954da38ad898e7b30ecba0`.\r\n\r\nPickle streams were decoded with a primitive-only pickletools interpreter that rejects executable/global/reduction opcodes, not unrestricted pickle loading. Streaming scans aggregate count, mean, population standard deviation, minimum and maximum into calendar minutes. Nonfinite and -9999 numeric fields are excluded; all four CSAT3 diagnostic bits must be false. Horizontal speed is the sample mean of sqrt(ux²+uy²), not the magnitude of a block-mean vector. CO2 diagnostics are retained, not treated as zero-is-good sonic flags. Negative CO2 values remain visibly flagged for review.\r\n\r\n## Supporting files and references\r\n- `konza-aligned-minutes.csv`: complete April 15–18 calendar-minute grid, particle means/ranges/counts/gap status, wind record/flag counts and component/speed statistics, dry CO2 means/ranges/counts and diagnostic fields. No interpolation or clock shifting. Wind component/speed units are m/s; CO2MFd units are µmol/mol. Missing high-frequency values after April 17 are not zeros.\r\n- `konza-time-of-day.csv`: corrected explorer grid and midpoint filter key. `konza-particle-pattern.csv`: minute summaries plus a separately identified raw peak window; do not average mixed grains together. `konza-sampling-gaps.csv`: exact adjacent particle timestamp intervals longer than 3 seconds.\r\n- `konza-cross-sensor-coverage.csv`: six streams in ten-minute bins; record presence is not quality approval. `konza-provenance.json`: source and derivative SHA-256 hashes, source profiles and boundary regression results.\r\n- Working programs are retained under `.tmp/clio-documents/`: primitive_pickle.py, scan_high_frequency.py (wind path), scan_li7200_packets.py, analyze_aligned.py, fix_boundary.py and build_provenance.py. The scans retain full-record minute summaries and profiles there.\r\n\r\nManufacturer and method sources:\r\n1. [ES-642 datasheet, pp. 1–2](https://metone.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/02/ES-642-datasheet.pdf), optical measurement, inlet options and sensitivity.\r\n2. [Contemporaneous Sage plugin source](https://github.com/waggle-sensor/plugin-air-quality/blob/85910d2d187d0bbdb5a93c6d9aac28e6d46e940e/main.py), concentration pass-through and published fields; a contemporaneous source, not a verified container digest match.\r\n3. [Bosch BME680 datasheet, especially §4.2](https://www.bosch-sensortec.com/media/boschsensortec/downloads/datasheets/bst-bme680-ds001.pdf), resistance response and measurement limitations.\r\n4. [LI-7200/RS manufacturer manual](https://licor.app.boxenterprise.net/shared/static/k8b90wiwdhietarx04qd), physical PDF p. 159 (§8-11) for DiagVal and p. 288 (E-10) for CO2MFd/CO2D mapping; [diagnostic conventions](https://www.licor.com/support/LI-7200RS/topics/diagnostics-eddypro.html), including CSAT3 bits 12–15. The manual defines channels, not this deployment's calibration history.\r\n5. [NEON wind ATBD revision B](https://data.neonscience.org/api/v0/documents/NEON.DOC.000780vB), raw components and orientation requirements.\r\n6. [Experiment exporter notebook](https://github.com/sagecontinuum/neon-kafka-exporter/blob/main/burn-event/post-processing/exploratory-data-analysis.ipynb), contextual example only; actual coverage above comes from downloaded bytes, not notebook figures.\r\n\r\n## LI-7200 packet-count audit\r\nThe initial archive-row pass is superseded. Some archive records contain multiple complete `(Data ...)` packets. The first pass collapsed repeated field names into one dictionary, keeping the last packet's values but using the archive row's readout_time. Thus its CO2 counts were archive-record counts, not measurement-packet counts, and some concentrations could be assigned an earlier packet's time.\r\n\r\nThe replacement scanner parses each balanced Data packet independently, retaining that packet's Date, Time, Ndx, values and diagnostics. Measurements are binned by their own embedded Date/Time rather than one timestamp for the containing archive record. Both observations are used; a packet is not dropped simply because it shares an archive record. Counts of archive records, all packets and unique measurement timestamps are reported separately. The immutable raw archive remains available for packet-level reconstruction; the minute derivative is an aggregate, not a lossless raw-data substitute. Embedded times are interpreted as UTC consistently with their matching readout_time; independent cross-instrument clock synchronization remains unverified.\r\n\r\nExample from archive record 915: outer readout_time is 2022-04-15 12:00:45.600 UTC. It contains Ndx 180690197 at 12:00:45.600 with CO2MFd 426.514 µmol/mol, and Ndx 180690212 at 12:00:45.650 with CO2MFd 426.502. The earlier pass kept 426.502 at .600 and lost 426.514. The corrected pass keeps both at their respective times, 50 ms apart. This illustrates why archive-row completeness cannot establish a measurement-time gap or sample count.\r\n\r\nThe full scan found 4,679,034 single-packet records and 482 double-packet records, totaling 4,679,998 packets and 4,679,998 unique measurement times. There were no missing packet Date/Time or CO2MFd fields, duplicate measurement timestamps or backward packet-time transitions. Of the extra packets, 480 were 50 ms later than the containing record readout_time and 2 were 100 ms later. All packets were included; no deduplication was needed. Counts changed in 458 minute bins. The 16:41 minute increases from 1,199 archive rows to 1,200 packets; its mean changes from 455.097306 to 455.097548 µmol/mol. The key table values are unchanged at two decimals. The maximum absolute minute-mean correction anywhere is 0.246949 µmol/mol, in the unresolved April 17 09:30 CDT interval. This correction does not resolve calibration or cross-instrument timing uncertainty.\r\n\r\n\nThe aligned export explicitly separates `co2_packets` / `CO2MFd_n` (embedded measurement time) from `co2_archive_records_by_readout_time` (outer archive timestamp). The packet-count CSV compares both definitions for every covered minute. April 15 minute-mean corrections are at most 0.000769 µmol/mol; the rounded key figures and qualitative interpretation are unchanged. Particle gaps and the half-open explorer boundary correction are unaffected by this CO2 parser fix.\r\n\n## Updated context-view review\nThe corrected three-panel particle/CO2/wind comparison and six-stream coverage were rendered locally and visually inspected at approximately 740–770 pixels wide. The comparison retains the low-particle/high-CO2 counterexample around 16:22; it does not select only the matching peak. The shared time axes, symlog particle scale, linear gas/wind scales and observed min–max ranges were checked. Client inspection resolved the corrected comparison to 60 rows and coverage to 3,240 rows. A whole-client capture timed out; component capture then found a hidden/busy viewer. The inspected viewer had a very narrow 183-pixel width. Therefore the revised context view is not claimed client-pixel-verified or readable at that narrow size; expand it for plotting. This limitation does not apply to the separately captured and reviewed corrected particle-window explorer.\r\n","label":"","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"uri":"","command":"","timed_out":false,"status":"","detail":"","items":[],"action_label":""}]},"duration_ms":364.96996879577637,"started_at":"2026-10-19T18:24:14.965166+00:00","completed_at":"2026-10-19T18:24:15.330136+00:00"},{"id":"call_4b84d7774f83","session_id":"sess_b1bbf58c66a5","run_id":"6dfdf0f1-d306-4446-aabc-81dcfcde0d00","name":"fs_read_file","title":"Read File","state":"succeeded","input":{"_clio_task_description":"Read wind and CO2 limitations","access_reason":"","filepath":"D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza\\konza-wind-co2-coverage.md"},"output":{"content":[],"structuredContent":{"content":"# Wind and CO2 coverage and packet audit\n\nThis note supersedes the earlier large-file access-limit conclusion. Both large files were downloaded successfully from NDP and scanned in full. See konza-burn-analysis.md for the colleague summary and causal limitations, and konza-provenance.json for source identities and SHA-256 hashes.\n\n## Actual inspected coverage\n| Stream | Archive records | Measurement coverage in CDT |\n|---|---:|---|\n| CSAT3 | 4,680,001 | Just before April 15 07:00 to April 17 23:59:59 |\n| LI-7200 | 4,679,516 | Same span, 4,679,998 distinct Data-packet measurement times |\n| WindObserver II | 143,369 | April 16 08:10 to April 17 23:59 |\n| LI-840A | 138,807 | One isolated April 14 record, then April 16 09:24 to April 17 23:59 |\n| Picarro G2131-i | 151,870 | April 16 09:38 to April 17 23:59; nonempty buffers start around 10:02 April 16 |\n\nOnly CSAT3 and LI-7200 among these files cover April 15. The other three cannot corroborate burn-day events; their absence does not disprove a smoke interpretation.\n\n## Archive records are not measurement counts\nLI-7200 has 4,679,034 single-packet and 482 double-packet archive records. All 4,679,998 packets have distinct embedded measurement times, with no missing Date/Time or CO2MFd fields, duplicates or backward transitions. The 482 additional packets are 50 ms later than their archive row timestamp in 480 cases and 100 ms later in 2 cases. The largest observed packet-time gap is 0.100 s; the largest archive-record gap is 0.150 s.\n\nThe original one-dictionary-per-archive-row scan overwrote earlier values with later-packet values. It is superseded. The corrected scan parses each balanced Data packet independently and bins by its own Date/Time, interpreted as UTC. Both packets contribute to means and counts; no deduplication was necessary. Raw archive bytes remain read-only and lossless; the minute CSV is an aggregate, not a replacement for raw packets.\r\n\n## Effect on the burn comparison\nCounts change in 458 minute bins. The 16:41 CDT peak minute on April 15 changes from 1,199 archive rows to 1,200 CO2 packets, with mean 455.097306 → 455.097548 µmol/mol. Its reported value remains 455.10 at two decimals. Across April 15 the maximum absolute minute-mean correction is 0.000769 µmol/mol; across the whole archive it is 0.246949 in the unresolved April 17 09:30 CDT interval. The packet correction is essential for honest counts, precise timing and future covariance analysis even though the rounded burn-day table and qualitative conclusion are unchanged.\n\nCorrected CO2 measurements are not a validated combustion tracer or flux product. Nominal DiagVal 8191 and DiagVal2 0 coexist with negative CO2 intervals on April 16 and 17, whose operational/calibration cause remains unresolved. CSAT3 statistics exclude all 256 flagged wind samples, including 128 of 1,200 in the 16:41 minute. North orientation, geometry, inlet lag and independent clock synchronization remain unverified.\n\n## Files and definitions\n- konza-aligned-minutes.csv retains `co2_packets` and `CO2MFd_n` by embedded measurement time, separately from `co2_archive_records_by_readout_time`. Particle gaps and wind flag counts are retained. It is a calendar-minute aggregate, not a packet-level raw export.\n- konza-co2-packet-count-audit.csv compares old and corrected counts and means for all 3,901 covered calendar-minute bins, including the partial first minute.\n- konza-cross-sensor-coverage.csv uses `count_basis` to distinguish LI-7200 packets, other sensor archive records and W038 particle readings.\n- konza-provenance.json contains raw hashes, profiles, packet multiplicity, offset distribution, representative dual-packet records, synthetic minute-boundary regression and reconciled export totals.\n\nThe exact LI-7200 raw archive SHA-256 is `08864a5c391aed5b4664b3c106055cda3fdb8e74fd29ecd617c2a0f1b6144044`; CSAT3 is `67ee10072ff2aa284f29465a86bcdb5db059ee333d3f3929f2a6cbaf356f289d`. Both belong to NDP dataset `335fa1cb-d6d2-4e96-bc8e-c309d957b24d`, resources `8a6fc572-d84b-4c2f-9f92-e141d7e99687` and `07a135d1-bcd3-4e12-aff5-1e91d667fe5e` respectively.\n\n[Manufacturer manual](https://licor.app.boxenterprise.net/shared/static/k8b90wiwdhietarx04qd), physical PDF p. 288 (E-10), defines CO2MFd as dry mole fraction (µmol/mol), distinct from CO2D density (mmol/m³); p. 159 (§8-11) defines diagnostics. These definitions do not validate this deployment's calibration history.\r\n\n## Presentation verification\nEquivalent corrected charts were rendered locally and visually inspected at about 740–770 pixels wide. Client queries resolved 60 aligned minutes and 3,240 coverage cells. Whole-view capture timed out; later component capture found hidden/busy viewer state. The narrow mounted target was 183 pixels wide, so client readability at that size is not verified. Use the expanded view for the comparison. The separately corrected particle explorer had successful matching client captures.\r\n","path":"D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza\\konza-wind-co2-coverage.md","size_bytes":4960,"truncated":false}},"presentation":{"action":"Read","subject":"file-link","summary":"4960 bytes","blocks":[{"id":"file-link","type":"link","media_type":"","text":"","label":"konza-wind-co2-coverage.md","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"target":"file","uri":"D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza\\konza-wind-co2-coverage.md","command":"","timed_out":false,"status":"","detail":"","items":[],"action_label":""},{"id":"file","type":"markdown","media_type":"","text":"# Wind and CO2 coverage and packet audit\n\nThis note supersedes the earlier large-file access-limit conclusion. Both large files were downloaded successfully from NDP and scanned in full. See konza-burn-analysis.md for the colleague summary and causal limitations, and konza-provenance.json for source identities and SHA-256 hashes.\n\n## Actual inspected coverage\n| Stream | Archive records | Measurement coverage in CDT |\n|---|---:|---|\n| CSAT3 | 4,680,001 | Just before April 15 07:00 to April 17 23:59:59 |\n| LI-7200 | 4,679,516 | Same span, 4,679,998 distinct Data-packet measurement times |\n| WindObserver II | 143,369 | April 16 08:10 to April 17 23:59 |\n| LI-840A | 138,807 | One isolated April 14 record, then April 16 09:24 to April 17 23:59 |\n| Picarro G2131-i | 151,870 | April 16 09:38 to April 17 23:59; nonempty buffers start around 10:02 April 16 |\n\nOnly CSAT3 and LI-7200 among these files cover April 15. The other three cannot corroborate burn-day events; their absence does not disprove a smoke interpretation.\n\n## Archive records are not measurement counts\nLI-7200 has 4,679,034 single-packet and 482 double-packet archive records. All 4,679,998 packets have distinct embedded measurement times, with no missing Date/Time or CO2MFd fields, duplicates or backward transitions. The 482 additional packets are 50 ms later than their archive row timestamp in 480 cases and 100 ms later in 2 cases. The largest observed packet-time gap is 0.100 s; the largest archive-record gap is 0.150 s.\n\nThe original one-dictionary-per-archive-row scan overwrote earlier values with later-packet values. It is superseded. The corrected scan parses each balanced Data packet independently and bins by its own Date/Time, interpreted as UTC. Both packets contribute to means and counts; no deduplication was necessary. Raw archive bytes remain read-only and lossless; the minute CSV is an aggregate, not a replacement for raw packets.\r\n\n## Effect on the burn comparison\nCounts change in 458 minute bins. The 16:41 CDT peak minute on April 15 changes from 1,199 archive rows to 1,200 CO2 packets, with mean 455.097306 → 455.097548 µmol/mol. Its reported value remains 455.10 at two decimals. Across April 15 the maximum absolute minute-mean correction is 0.000769 µmol/mol; across the whole archive it is 0.246949 in the unresolved April 17 09:30 CDT interval. The packet correction is essential for honest counts, precise timing and future covariance analysis even though the rounded burn-day table and qualitative conclusion are unchanged.\n\nCorrected CO2 measurements are not a validated combustion tracer or flux product. Nominal DiagVal 8191 and DiagVal2 0 coexist with negative CO2 intervals on April 16 and 17, whose operational/calibration cause remains unresolved. CSAT3 statistics exclude all 256 flagged wind samples, including 128 of 1,200 in the 16:41 minute. North orientation, geometry, inlet lag and independent clock synchronization remain unverified.\n\n## Files and definitions\n- konza-aligned-minutes.csv retains `co2_packets` and `CO2MFd_n` by embedded measurement time, separately from `co2_archive_records_by_readout_time`. Particle gaps and wind flag counts are retained. It is a calendar-minute aggregate, not a packet-level raw export.\n- konza-co2-packet-count-audit.csv compares old and corrected counts and means for all 3,901 covered calendar-minute bins, including the partial first minute.\n- konza-cross-sensor-coverage.csv uses `count_basis` to distinguish LI-7200 packets, other sensor archive records and W038 particle readings.\n- konza-provenance.json contains raw hashes, profiles, packet multiplicity, offset distribution, representative dual-packet records, synthetic minute-boundary regression and reconciled export totals.\n\nThe exact LI-7200 raw archive SHA-256 is `08864a5c391aed5b4664b3c106055cda3fdb8e74fd29ecd617c2a0f1b6144044`; CSAT3 is `67ee10072ff2aa284f29465a86bcdb5db059ee333d3f3929f2a6cbaf356f289d`. Both belong to NDP dataset `335fa1cb-d6d2-4e96-bc8e-c309d957b24d`, resources `8a6fc572-d84b-4c2f-9f92-e141d7e99687` and `07a135d1-bcd3-4e12-aff5-1e91d667fe5e` respectively.\n\n[Manufacturer manual](https://licor.app.boxenterprise.net/shared/static/k8b90wiwdhietarx04qd), physical PDF p. 288 (E-10), defines CO2MFd as dry mole fraction (µmol/mol), distinct from CO2D density (mmol/m³); p. 159 (§8-11) defines diagnostics. These definitions do not validate this deployment's calibration history.\r\n\n## Presentation verification\nEquivalent corrected charts were rendered locally and visually inspected at about 740–770 pixels wide. Client queries resolved 60 aligned minutes and 3,240 coverage cells. Whole-view capture timed out; later component capture found hidden/busy viewer state. The narrow mounted target was 183 pixels wide, so client readability at that size is not verified. Use the expanded view for the comparison. The separately corrected particle explorer had successful matching client captures.\r\n","label":"","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"uri":"","command":"","timed_out":false,"status":"","detail":"","items":[],"action_label":""}]},"duration_ms":361.522912979126,"started_at":"2026-10-19T18:24:14.977420+00:00","completed_at":"2026-10-19T18:24:15.338943+00:00"},{"id":"call_565e7a86d8c5","session_id":"sess_b1bbf58c66a5","run_id":"6dfdf0f1-d306-4446-aabc-81dcfcde0d00","name":"fs_read_file","title":"Read File","state":"succeeded","input":{"_clio_task_description":"Inspect source provenance and measurement units","access_reason":"","filepath":"D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza\\konza-provenance.json"},"output":{"content":[],"structuredContent":{"content":"{\r\n  \"dataset_id\": \"335fa1cb-d6d2-4e96-bc8e-c309d957b24d\",\r\n  \"title\": \"NEON MDP / Sage / WIFIRE BP3D: Konza Prairie Burn Experiment\",\r\n  \"timezone\": \"America/Chicago\",\r\n  \"interval_semantics\": \"start inclusive, end exclusive; calendar minute midpoints for supported control endpoints\",\r\n  \"resource_ids\": {\r\n    \"21-W038.ndjson\": \"4e1ccb5d-aef9-4c06-846a-e45eb6d72fd1\",\r\n    \"1-reading.sensor.csat3.pkl\": \"07a135d1-bcd3-4e12-aff5-1e91d667fe5e\",\r\n    \"7-reading.sensor.li7200_raw.pkl\": \"8a6fc572-d84b-4c2f-9f92-e141d7e99687\",\r\n    \"17-reading.sensor.windobserverii.pkl\": \"ac7e07d8-a67c-4025-a5bf-3ccf9c324d4b\",\r\n    \"8-reading.sensor.li840a.pkl\": \"db35cec6-e8b1-4543-b203-b301fdc99a14\",\r\n    \"2-reading.sensor.g2131i_raw.pkl\": \"90d20433-16ca-48ed-a44f-dbebce72f473\"\r\n  },\r\n  \"lower_frequency_profiles\": {\r\n    \"17-reading.sensor.windobserverii.pkl\": {\r\n      \"rows\": 143369,\r\n      \"start\": \"2022-04-16T13:10:06.935000+00:00\",\r\n      \"end\": \"2022-04-18T04:59:59.292000+00:00\",\r\n      \"nulls\": {},\r\n      \"samples\": [\r\n        {\r\n          \"source_id\": \"50274\",\r\n          \"site_id\": \"MDP5\",\r\n          \"readout_time\": \"2022-04-16T13:10:06.935000+00:00\",\r\n   