0419922,"started_at":"2026-10-19T18:28:27.380185+00:00","completed_at":"2026-10-19T18:28:27.454712+00:00"},{"id":"call_5ad539a94b41","session_id":"sess_b1bbf58c66a5","run_id":"42a357b3-2c57-44d4-95ab-a2dcf2db03f3","name":"shell_bash","title":"Bash","state":"succeeded","input":{"_clio_task_description":"Confirm fields, units and burn-day context for time-window comparison","access_paths":null,"access_reason":"","background":false,"command":"Get-Content konza-aligned-minutes.csv -TotalCount 2; Get-Content konza-burn-analysis.md -TotalCount 28","cwd":"D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza","max_output_bytes":4000,"timeout_s":30},"output":{"content":[],"structuredContent":{"command":"Get-Content konza-aligned-minutes.csv -TotalCount 2; Get-Content konza-burn-analysis.md -TotalCount 28","cwd":"D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza","exit_code":0,"stderr":"","stderr_truncated":false,"stdout":"timestamp_utc,date_local,local_hour,hour_end,bucket_mid_hour,local_label,particle_mean_ug_m3,particle_median_ug_m3,particle_min_ug_m3,particle_max_ug_m3,particle_n,particle_status,gap_seconds,wind_records,wind_flagged,horizontal_speed_n,horizontal_speed_mean,horizontal_speed_min,horizontal_speed_max,ux_wind_speed_mean,uy_wind_speed_mean,uz_wind_speed_mean,co2_packets,CO2MFd_n,CO2MFd_mean,CO2MFd_min,CO2MFd_max,CO2SS_mean,DiagVal_min,DiagVal_max,DiagVal2_min,DiagVal2_max,co2_review,co2_archive_records_by_readout_time\n2022-04-15T05:00:00+00:00,2022-04-15,0.0,0.0166666666666666,0.0083333333333333,Apr 15 00:00 CDT,,,,,0,Outside record,0.0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,No records,\n# Konza burn observations and what they support\n\n## Colleague summary\nOn April 15, 2022, the W038 ES-642 recorded large, intermittent particle bursts, with lower-level elevation persisting into the evening. This is consistent with smoke-bearing air intermittently reaching W038 during the burn experiment. It is not proof of ignition time, the cause of every burst, prairie-wide exposure or a health-standard exceedance.\n\nThe previously unread large files have now been downloaded and scanned in full. Both CSAT3 wind and LI-7200 CO2 cover the burn day. Their observations provide context, but do not establish a plume trajectory, a calm-air accumulation mechanism, a combustion-specific CO2 enhancement, an emission factor or an eddy-covariance flux.\n\nAll local times use America/Chicago, CDT (UTC-05:00). Every interval is start-inclusive and end-exclusive. Reported averages are sample-weighted, not gap-adjusted exposure estimates.\n\n## Key measured figures\n| April 15 CDT interval | Particle mean (�g/m3) | LI-7200 dry CO2 mean (�mol/mol) | Screened CSAT3 horizontal speed mean (m/s) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 08:00-11:00 | 1.43 | 435.27 | 4.94 |\n| 12:19-12:20 | 764.85 | 443.87 | 7.10 |\n| 15:00-16:00 | 3.18 | 449.95 | 6.01 |\n| 16:30-17:00 | 450.85 | 454.18 | 6.25 |\n| 16:41-16:42 | 3,538.36 | 455.10 | 7.35 |\n| 18:32-18:33 | 864.76 | 451.83 | 7.25 |\n| 19:00-20:00 | 7.36 | 445.91 | 5.55 |\n\nThe largest individual particle reading was **12,758 �g/m3 at 16:41:42.748 CDT**. The 16:41 minute contains 55 particle readings, 1,200 LI-7200 Data packets and 1,200 CSAT3 records; 128 flagged wind records were excluded, leaving 1,072 for its speed statistic. Min-max ranges in the views are observed variability, not confidence intervals.\n\n## What we can conclude\n- The particle change was episodic, not a smooth all-day rise. April 15 has 51,918 recorded readings from 08:00 to midnight: 199 (0.38%) exceed 1,000 �g/m3 and 2,573 (4.96%) exceed 100 �g/m3. These are descriptive sample fractions, not health thresholds or fractions of elapsed time.\n- Lower-level elevation also lingered. The 16:00-19:00 mean/median was 132.69/29 �g/m3; 19:00-24:00 was 7.73/6, compared with 1.43/1 at 08:00-11:00. Block summaries do not prove a continuously elevated plateau through unobserved intervals.\n- CO2 shows a modest local rise near the main particle burst. Its largest April 15 minute mean was 455.79 �mol/mol at 16:43, compared with 453.21 at 16:35. This is descriptive temporal concurrence, not a lag estimate or source attribution. A near-equal CO2 mean of 455.25 occurred at 16:22 while particles averaged only 2.60 �g/m3, so the two signals are not simply proportional.\n- The screened horizontal wind-speed mean at the particle-peak minute was 7.35 m/s, versus 6.01 over 15:00-16:00. These observations do not support a simple calm-air explanation. They do not rule out effects of turbulence, stability, source motion or spatially varying dispersion.\n\n","stdout_truncated":false,"timed_out":false,"timeout_s":30.0}},"presentation":{"action":"Run","summary":"","blocks":[{"id":"terminal","type":"terminal","media_type":"","text":"timestamp_utc,date_local,local_hour,hour_end,bucket_mid_hour,local_label,particle_mean_ug_m3,particle_median_ug_m3,particle_min_ug_m3,particle_max_ug_m3,particle_n,particle_status,gap_seconds,wind_records,wind_flagged,horizontal_speed_n,horizontal_speed_mean,horizontal_speed_min,horizontal_speed_max,ux_wind_speed_mean,uy_wind_speed_mean,uz_wind_speed_mean,co2_packets,CO2MFd_n,CO2MFd_mean,CO2MFd_min,CO2MFd_max,CO2SS_mean,DiagVal_min,DiagVal_max,DiagVal2_min,DiagVal2_max,co2_review,co2_archive_records_by_readout_time\n2022-04-15T05:00:00+00:00,2022-04-15,0.0,0.0166666666666666,0.0083333333333333,Apr 15 00:00 CDT,,,,,0,Outside record,0.0,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,No records,\n# Konza burn observations and what they support\n\n## Colleague summary\nOn April 15, 2022, the W038 ES-642 recorded large, intermittent particle bursts, with lower-level elevation persisting into the evening. This is consistent with smoke-bearing air intermittently reaching W038 during the burn experiment. It is not proof of ignition time, the cause of every burst, prairie-wide exposure or a health-standard exceedance.\n\nThe previously unread large files have now been downloaded and scanned in full. Both CSAT3 wind and LI-7200 CO2 cover the burn day. Their observations provide context, but do not establish a plume trajectory, a calm-air accumulation mechanism, a combustion-specific CO2 enhancement, an emission factor or an eddy-covariance flux.\n\nAll local times use America/Chicago, CDT (UTC-05:00). Every interval is start-inclusive and end-exclusive. Reported averages are sample-weighted, not gap-adjusted exposure estimates.\n\n## Key measured figures\n| April 15 CDT interval | Particle mean (�g/m3) | LI-7200 dry CO2 mean (�mol/mol) | Screened CSAT3 horizontal speed mean (m/s) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 08:00-11:00 | 1.43 | 435.27 | 4.94 |\n| 12:19-12:20 | 764.85 | 443.87 | 7.10 |\n| 15:00-16:00 | 3.18 | 449.95 | 6.01 |\n| 16:30-17:00 | 450.85 | 454.18 | 6.25 |\n| 16:41-16:42 | 3,538.36 | 455.10 | 7.35 |\n| 18:32-18:33 | 864.76 | 451.83 | 7.25 |\n| 19:00-20:00 | 7.36 | 445.91 | 5.55 |\n\nThe largest individual particle reading was **12,758 �g/m3 at 16:41:42.748 CDT**. The 16:41 minute contains 55 particle readings, 1,200 LI-7200 Data packets and 1,200 CSAT3 records; 128 flagged wind records were excluded, leaving 1,072 for its speed statistic. Min-max ranges in the views are observed variability, not confidence intervals.\n\n## What we can conclude\n- The particle change was episodic, not a smooth all-day rise. April 15 has 51,918 recorded readings from 08:00 to midnight: 199 (0.38%) exceed 1,000 �g/m3 and 2,573 (4.96%) exceed 100 �g/m3. These are descriptive sample fractions, not health thresholds or fractions of elapsed time.\n- Lower-level elevation also lingered. The 16:00-19:00 mean/median was 132.69/29 �g/m3; 19:00-24:00 was 7.73/6, compared with 1.43/1 at 08:00-11:00. Block summaries do not prove a continuously elevated plateau through unobserved intervals.\n- CO2 shows a modest local rise near the main particle burst. Its largest April 15 minute mean was 455.79 �mol/mol at 16:43, compared with 453.21 at 16:35. This is descriptive temporal concurrence, not a lag estimate or source attribution. A near-equal CO2 mean of 455.25 occurred at 16:22 while particles averaged only 2.60 �g/m3, so the two signals are not simply proportional.\n- The screened horizontal wind-speed mean at the particle-peak minute was 7.35 m/s, versus 6.01 over 15:00-16:00. These observations do not support a simple calm-air explanation. They do not rule out effects of turbulence, stability, source motion or spatially varying dispersion.\n\n","label":"","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"uri":"","command":"Get-Content konza-aligned-minutes.csv -TotalCount 2; Get-Content konza-burn-analysis.md -TotalCount 28","timed_out":false,"status":"","detail":"","items":[],"action_label":"","exit_code":0}]},"duration_ms":2267.1358585357666,"started_at":"2026-10-19T18:29:25.829706+00:00","completed_at":"2026-10-19T18:29:28.096842+00:00"},{"id":"call_5bbb0b32da7d","session_id":"sess_b1bbf58c66a5","run_id":"42a357b3-2c57-44d4-95ab-a2dcf2db03f3","name":"write_todos","title":"Update Todos","state":"succeeded","input":{"todos":[{"content":"Calculate paired particle and CO2 correlations, peak ranks and time-window sensitivity","status":"in_progress"},{"content":"Present and review a focused comparison view with exclusions and causal limits","status":"pending"}]},"output":"Recorded 2 todo(s): 0 completed, 1 in_progress, 1 pending.","presentation":{"action":"Update Todos","summary":"5 todos changed","blocks":[{"id":"todo-0","type":"check","media_type":"","text":"Calculate paired particle and CO2 correlations, peak ranks and time-window sensitivity","label":"","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"state":"in_progress","change":"added","uri":"","command":"","timed_out":false,"status":"","detail":"","items":[],"action_label":""},{"id":"todo-1","type":"check","media_type":"","text":"Present and review a focused comparison view with exclusions and causal limits","label":"","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"state":"pending","change":"added","uri":"","