"kwargs":{"path":"D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza\\.tmp\\clio-documents\\figure-2-association-review.png"}},"output":{"media_type":"image/png","path":".tmp/clio-documents/figure-2-association-review.png","sha256":"4c9b13d69898d1e11954de5bbbbbf03aeca1e72f6943bc727ee1bf94f169adc6","size_bytes":167956,"snapshot":"clio-tool-output:sess_b1bbf58c66a5/viewed-4c9b13d69898d1e11954de5b.png","snapshot_sha256":"4c9b13d69898d1e11954de5bbbbbf03aeca1e72f6943bc727ee1bf94f169adc6","type":"clio.workspace_image.v1"},"presentation":{"summary":"","blocks":[{"id":"workspace-file","type":"workspace_file","media_type":"image/png","text":"","label":"","language":"","workspace_id":"ws_6978effdcd75","path":".tmp/clio-documents/figure-2-association-review.png","sha256":"4c9b13d69898d1e11954de5bbbbbf03aeca1e72f6943bc727ee1bf94f169adc6","pages":[],"uri":"","command":"","timed_out":false,"status":"","detail":"","items":[],"action_label":""}]},"duration_ms":2546.1723804473877,"started_at":"2026-10-19T18:36:01.253722+00:00","completed_at":"2026-10-19T18:36:03.799895+00:00"},{"id":"call_c8843d3e7d5e","session_id":"sess_b1bbf58c66a5","run_id":"a2120cfd-46d0-4d78-8f53-40d5d0754a9e","name":"create_artifact","title":"Create Artifact","state":"succeeded","input":{"annotation":"Concise collaborator handoff separating robust recorded features, sensitivity results, unresolved sensor limits and prioritized next measurements.","artifacts":null,"content":"# Konza burn observations for a collaborator\n\n**Scope:** April 15, 2022 burn-day comparisons, America/Chicago CDT (UTC−05:00). Historical observations from NDP dataset `335fa1cb-d6d2-4e96-bc8e-c309d957b24d`, *NEON MDP / Sage / WIFIRE BP3D: Konza Prairie Burn Experiment*. W038 ES-642 particles are compared with MDP5 CSAT3 wind and LI-7200 dry CO₂. These are different instruments; co-location and sampling geometry are not established.\n\n## What is robust within the inspected records\n\n- **Particle bursts are a real feature of the recorded optical signal, not a smooth all-day rise.** Morning 08:00–11:00 mean was 1.43 µg/m³; the 16:41 minute mean was 3,538.36, with an individual maximum of 12,758 at 16:41:42.748. This is consistent with intermittent smoke reaching W038, not proof of the cause of every burst. Absolute smoke mass is not independently calibrated.\n- **The peak does not fit a simple calm-air accumulation account.** Screened horizontal wind averaged 7.35 m/s in the peak minute versus 6.01 during 15:00–16:00. This local speed comparison does not establish transport direction, turbulence, stability or conditions at the source.\n- **Particles and CO₂ are not interchangeable tracers.** Only four of the ten highest particle minutes were also top-ten CO₂ minutes among 958 paired minutes from 08:00–24:00. At 16:23, CO₂ ranked fifth (455.26 µmol/mol), but particles ranked 505th (3.52 µg/m³). Adjacent top minutes often belong to one burst, not independent events.\n- **A single correlation is not stable across analysis choices.** Pearson r is 0.196 for 08:00–24:00, 0.438 for 16:00–17:00 and −0.522 for 15:00–16:00. Spearman ρ changes from 0.798 for 08:00–20:00 to 0.297 when extending to midnight. Full-window Pearson r rises to 0.319 with 15-minute averaging and 0.494 with hourly averaging. Coarser averaging emphasizes broader changes, not stronger causal evidence.\n- **Basic gap sensitivity does not explain the weak full-window linear association.** Removing 32 partially gap-affected minutes changes r from 0.196 (n=958) to 0.195 (n=926). Corrected LI-7200 packet parsing changes April 15 minute means by at most 0.000769 µmol/mol; rounded burn-day findings are unchanged.\n\n## What remains unresolved\n\nES-642 has 1 µg/m³ sensitivity; inlet size cut, smoke calibration and full particle status screening are unavailable. Do not label the signal confirmed PM2.5/PM10, calculate AQI or interpret precise morning-to-peak ratios. Across its 65-hour record, 12 whole particle minutes are empty and the longest inter-reading interval is 73.99 s. Two empty minutes occur in the burn-day paired window. No interpolation was applied.\n\nCSAT3 statistics exclude 256 flagged samples, including 128 of 1,200 in the peak minute. LI-7200 nominal diagnostics do not certify ambient sampling: negative values occur April 16 10:00–10:06 and April 17 09:30–09:36, with unresolved operational/calibration causes. They are retained and flagged in the full grid, but those days are not included in the correlation comparison. WindObserver II, LI-840A and Picarro do not cover April 15.\n\nUnknown geometry, north orientation, independent clock synchronization and inlet delays prevent defensible plume-path or lag inference. Particle times are publication/receipt times. No combustion-specific CO₂ enhancement, emission factor, regional exposure or eddy-covariance flux is established. Serial dependence and overlapping windows mean correlations are descriptive, without independent-sample significance claims.\n\n## Highest-value next evidence\n\n**First recover existing deployment records:** survey coordinates/heights and north orientation, inlet/tubing dimensions and flow, clock logs, zero/span and valve-state logs, ES-642 inlet specification and burn-operation timeline. Resolve negative CO₂ episodes and verify timing before attempting lag or covariance analysis; existing wind and CO₂ already sample near 20 Hz.\n\n| Priority for new measurements | Measurement | Main uncertainty reduced |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Synchronized upwind background and multiple downwind stations measuring CO₂, particles and a combustion tracer such as CO, with shared intake locations where feasible | Separate local background variation from plume enhancements and strengthen, not guarantee, burn attribution |\n| 2 | Surveyed north-referenced 3-D wind at relevant heights, temperature/stability observations and time-stamped fire-front maps or thermal imagery | Test whether intermittent source-to-sensor plume crossings explain bursts; distinguish transport and mixing alternatives |\n| 3 | Co-located size-resolved particles, reference filter mass, RH/temperature and pre/post-event calibrations | Establish size fraction, smoke optical response, humidity sensitivity and instrument validity before quantitative mass or exposure claims |\n\nIf emissions are the goal, additionally measure fuel consumption and burned area and validate the plume sampling/flux method. Concentration correlations alone cannot supply an emission factor.\n\n## Key figures and retained evidence\n\n1. **figure-1-timeline.png** shows 08:00–20:00 minute means, observed ranges and coverage flags. Ranges are not confidence intervals; records present is not quality approval.\n2. **figure-2-association.png** shows all 958 paired minutes, selected peak/counterexample labels and window-specific Pearson/Spearman comparisons. Its particle axis is symlog, but reported Pearson correlations use untransformed concentrations.\n\nThe package includes the complete aligned minute grid, paired-minute and window-correlation tables, exact particle gap intervals, source provenance, the packet-audit note and figure specifications. Within-minute and larger-bin means are sample-weighted; correlations weight each paired minute or aggregate bin equally. Windows are start-inclusive and end-exclusive; no lag shifts or background subtraction were applied. See `data/` and `methods/` for retained evidence and reproducible derivative calculations. Raw NDJSON/pickle archives are not included; their resource IDs and hashes are retained in the provenance file.\n","kind":"report","name":"D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza\\collaborator-handoff\\konza-collaborator-summary.md","path":"","pdf_preview":null,"purpose":"deliverable","used":["D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza\\konza-aligned-minutes.csv","D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza\\.tmp\\clio-documents\\konza-window-results.json","D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza\\konza-wind-co2-coverage.md","D:\\CLIO-Develop\\ndp-showcases-20261019\\konza\\konza-provenance.json"]},"output":{"artifacts":[{"accepted":true,"artifact_id":"artifact_7c90c3e27a3441f59062ba0e460139b5","created":true,"custody":"cas","kind":"report","mechanism":"model","name":"konza-collaborator-summary.md","reason":"","sha256":"92dec90ee39bebdd8df9fa205c35c3e723af2da7d3e7468ccaf90337e51c2f9e","version":1}],"created":1,"deduplicated":0,"pdf_previews":[],"promotion_budget":{"limit":8,"remaining":7,"used":1},"rejected":0},"presentation":{"action":"Create Artifact","subject":"artifact-0","summary":"Created version 1","blocks":[{"id":"artifact-0","type":"link","media_type":"","text":"","label":"konza-collaborator-summary.md","language":"","workspace_id":"","path":"","sha256":"","pages":[],"target":"artifact","uri":"artifact_7c90c3e27a3441f59062ba0e460139b5","command":"","timed_out":false,"status":"","detail":"","items":[],"action_label":""}]},"duration_ms":480.2272319793701,"started_at":"2026-10-19T18:36:49.612077+00:00","completed_at":"2026-10-19T18:36:50.092304+00:00"},{"id":"call_2132e2e0b7cc","session_id":"sess_b1bbf58c66a5","run_id":"a2120cfd-46d0-4d78-8f53-40d5d0754a9e","name":"shell_bash","title":"Bash","state":"succeeded","input":{"_clio_task_description":"Separate overlapping correlation labels and rerender final figures","access_paths":null,"access_reason":"","background":false,"command":"$p='.tmp/clio-documents/build_collaborator_figures.py'; $s=Get-Content $p -Raw; $s=$s.Replace(\"labels=b.mark_text(dx=9,align='left',fontSize=12).encode(text=alt.Text('estimate:Q',format='.2f'))\",\"labels=alt.layer(b.transform_filter(alt.datum.statistic=='Pearson r').mark_text(dx=8,dy=-8,align='left',fontSize=12).encode(text=alt.Text('estimate:Q',format='.2f')),b.transform_filter(alt.datum.statistic=='Spearman ρ').mark_text(dx=8,dy=9,align='left',fontSize=12).encode(text=alt.Text('estimate:Q',format='.2f')))\"); Set-Content -Encoding utf8 $p $s\n& 'D:\\Libraries\\Documents\